<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- DEBUG ONLY. Release builds never merge this file, so they keep Android's
     default: cleartext refused everywhere. That guarantee is the whole point of
     keeping this in src/debug rather than src/main, and it is why widening the
     rules here cannot mask a production mistake.

     Two things need plain HTTP while developing and demoing:

       10.0.2.2 / localhost   the local Supabase stand-in (dev/docker-compose.yml)
       the local network      the laptop serving lesson video (dev/video-server)

     The video server is the reason base-config is now permissive rather than a
     list of hosts. A network security config matches literal domains and IP
     addresses - it has no CIDR or wildcard form - and the laptop's address
     changes with every network it joins, so at a school it will not be the
     192.168.18.x it was at the office. Enumerating that is not possible; the
     alternative is a demo that dies with a silent cleartext exception on
     unfamiliar Wi-Fi, which looks exactly like the app being broken.

     If lesson video ever moves to HTTPS object storage, delete this file. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
